\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Pieter Bruegel the Elder, \textit {The Blind Leading the Blind} (1568).} Tempera on canvas, $86 \times 154$\,cm. Museo di Capodimonte, Naples. Commissioned in the year Bruegel died; completed just before the Spanish Duke of Alba imposed military occupation on the Netherlands. The background church (Sint-Anna-Pede, still standing near Brussels) either represents the only salvation the men are ignoring, or stands in silent indifference while its congregation falls.}}{xxxii}{figure.0.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Detail of \textit {The Blind Leading the Blind}, showing the two leading figures at the moment of fall.} The faces exhibit distinct ocular pathologies, not generic blindness. Medical historians have identified: corneal leucoma (opaque white scarring of the cornea), phthisis bulbi (the shrunken, atrophied globe of a long-extinguished eye), and surgical enucleation (the empty orbit of a removed eye). The upward tilt of the head---the blind man's compensatory posture, increasing auditory and olfactory field---is rendered with behavioural accuracy.}}{xxxiii}{figure.0.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {The van Aken workshop (conventionally attributed to Hieronymus Bosch), \textit {The Garden of Earthly Delights} (c.\ 1490--1510).} Oil on oak panel (central), oil on oak panel (wings). Central panel: $220 \times 195$\,cm; wings each $220 \times 97$\,cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid. Left wing: Paradise (the third day of creation). Centre: the Garden of Earthly Delights. Right wing: Hell. Exterior (not shown): the Creation of the World, rendered in grisaille (grey scale). Dendrochronological analysis of the oak support dates the earliest possible felling to 1460--1466. The painting bears no signature.}}{xxxv}{figure.0.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Pieter Bruegel the Elder (published under Hieronymus Bosch's name), \textit {Big Fish Eat Little Fish} (1556, published 1557).} Engraving, $22.9 \times 29.6$\,cm. Published by Hieronymus Cock, Antwerp, with the inscription ``Hieronymus Bos inventor'' (Hieronymus Bosch invented this)---a deliberate commercial misattribution. Bruegel was hired to produce work in the Boschian style; his employer sold it under the better-known name. The print depicts a vast fish beached on a shore, its belly being cut open to release a cascade of smaller fish, each of which is eating a still-smaller fish. The moral is ancient; the visual vocabulary is directly Boschian; the authorship is suppressed.}}{xli}{figure.0.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Pieter Bruegel the Elder (after), \textit {Luxuria} (Lust), from the series \textit {The Seven Deadly Sins} (1558).} Engraving. One of seven prints produced in the Boschian style for Hieronymus Cock, each depicting a deadly sin through the lens of Boschian grotesque imagery. These prints represent the most systematic translation of Bosch's spiritual vocabulary into Bruegel's secular, social idiom. Bruegel retained the creature-types and compositional strategies of the Boschian tradition while progressively replacing their theological referents with social and biological ones.}}{xlii}{figure.0.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Pieter Bruegel the Elder (circle of), \textit {Yawning Man} (c.\ 1563).} The transition from Boschian grotesque to Bruegel's observational naturalism is evident in the shift from symbolic creature-types to particularised human individuals. Where Bosch's figures are moral categories (the Fool, the Glutton, the Blind Man), Bruegel's figures are observed specimens. The yawning man is a man caught yawning---a particular human being in a particular involuntary moment---not a symbol of sloth. The same move underlies the clinical specificity of the eye conditions in \textit {The Blind Leading the Blind}.}}{xliii}{figure.0.6}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Triple Equivalence diagram showing the three mutually equivalent category representations of a dynamical system: oscillatory ($\mathbf {Osc}(\Omega )$), categorical ($\mathbf {Cat}(\Omega )$), and partition-theoretic ($\mathbf {Part}(\Omega )$). The functors $F_{OC}$, $F_{CP}$, and $F_{PO}$ form a commuting triangle, and S-values computed under each representation collapse onto the same identity line, confirming the three descriptions are epistemically interchangeable.}}{12}{figure.1.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~I summary: Foundations.} \textbf {(A)}~S-entropy space $\mathcal {S} = [0,1]^3$: 600 sampled states coloured by $S_k + S_t + S_e$. \textbf {(B)}~Triple Equivalence: S-values under oscillatory, categorical, and partition encodings collapse onto the identity line, confirming the three representations are equivalent up to irreducible floor noise. \textbf {(C)}~Cellular epistemic floor $\beta = \sigma /\!\sqrt {N}$ (log--log) for $N \in [10, 10^9]$; the shaded band spans $\sigma \in [0.5, 2.0]$. The floor is strictly positive across nine decades of $N$. \textbf {(D)}~Circular validation: three mutually supporting pillars (Bounded Phase Space, Categorical Observation, Triple Equivalence) with bidirectional support arrows; the shaded triangle quantifies the collective coherence threshold $> 0.5$.}}{17}{figure.1.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Circular validation and sufficient inclusions. The three mutually supporting pillars — Bounded Phase Space, Categorical Observation, and Triple Equivalence — are shown with bidirectional support arrows. The shaded triangle quantifies the collective coherence threshold ($>0.5$), illustrating why a size-3 circularly validating collection at threshold $\tau > \tfrac {1}{2}$ provides genuine foundational coherence, and why linear justification chains with cumulative floor $k\beta $ are epistemically inferior.}}{27}{figure.1.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Floor positivity and the S-functional. The epistemic floor $\beta (\mathcal {R}) = \varepsilon > 0$ is shown as a function of receiver resolution $\varepsilon $ (log--log scale) for ensemble sizes spanning nine decades. The shaded band illustrates the floor $\beta = \sigma /\!\sqrt {N}$ for $\sigma \in [0.5, 2.0]$. The floor is strictly positive across the entire range, confirming \Cref {thm:floor-positivity}: no bounded receiver can achieve $S(\mathcal {R}, x; C) = 0$ regardless of resolution refinement or repeated measurement.}}{31}{figure.2.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Cell-Truth and layered receivers. The left panel illustrates that truth about a system state $x$ is not a point but the positive-measure action-equivalence cell $[x]_\mathcal {R}$: all states $x'$ within this cell are S-indistinguishable, with $S(\mathcal {R}, x'; C) = \beta $ for all target cells $C$. The right panel shows a layered receiver hierarchy $\mathcal {R}_1 \prec \mathcal {R}_2 \prec \cdots \prec \mathcal {R}_k$ with monotonically decreasing but uniformly positive floors, demonstrating that the composite floor equals $\beta _k$ regardless of how many layers are composed.}}{33}{figure.2.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Group Blindness Theorem. The composite floor $\mathcal {S}_{\mathrm {flat}}(\mathcal {E}) = \DOTSB \prod@ \slimits@ _i \beta _i / (\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _i \beta _i)^{n-1}$ is plotted as a function of ensemble size $n$ for a homogeneous ensemble with $\beta _i = \beta $. The floor decays as $\beta / n^{n-1}$ toward zero asymptotically but remains strictly positive for every finite $n$. Inset: the multiplicative compounding of individual floors through catalytic composition, contrasting the geometric (composite) floor with the arithmetic average floor of \Cref {prop:ensemble-floor-lb}. No finite ensemble of bounded receivers achieves a collective floor of zero.}}{66}{figure.3.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Purpose as a fixed-point attractor. The ensemble dynamics $\Phi _\mathcal {E}$ is shown as a contraction mapping on the space of attainable cells $\mathcal {F}_{\mathrm {cells}}$ equipped with the Hausdorff metric $d_H$. Multiple trajectories from diverse initial cells $C_0$ converge geometrically to the unique fixed point $C^*$ (the purpose cell), with convergence rate $\lambda ^t$ where $\lambda < 1$ is the contraction factor from \Cref {prop:lipschitz-reaction}. The diagram illustrates that purpose is not a stored template but an $\omega $-limit set: the ensemble ``knows'' its purpose through the structure of its collective dynamics, not through any individual receiver's memory.}}{68}{figure.3.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~II summary: The Healthy Cell.} \textbf {(A)}~Kuramoto order parameter $r$ versus coupling strength $K$: the phase transition at $K_c = 1.6$ marks the onset of oscillatory synchrony underlying cellular phase-lock networks (\Cref {sec:phase-lock}). \textbf {(B)}~Proton conductance $G_H$ versus hydrogen-bond reorganisation time $\tau _{HB}$ (Grotthuss mechanism). High conductance requires fast bond reorganisation; the dashed line marks the physiological timescale relevant to \Cref {sec:proton-electron}. \textbf {(C)}~Enzyme catalytic efficiency $k_\mathrm {cat}/K_m$ versus partition depth $n$, peaking at intermediate depth $n = 7$ (\Cref {sec:aperture}). \textbf {(D)}~Three-dimensional synchrony landscape $r(K, N)$: order parameter as a function of coupling strength and ensemble size $N$.}}{99}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Phase-lock network structure in a biochemical Kuramoto ensemble. The left panel shows the synchronisation transition: the order parameter $r$ rises sharply from zero at the critical coupling $K_c = 2/(\pi g(0))$, marking the onset of coherent oscillation across the network. The right panel depicts the network topology: nodes (molecular species) are connected by edges weighted by coupling strength $K/N$; nodes coloured by current phase $\theta _i$ visualise the transition from incoherence ($r \approx 0$, uniformly distributed phases) to synchrony ($r \approx 1$, clustered phases). The physiological value $K_c \approx 1.6$ sets the health/disease phase boundary of \Cref {def:health-phase}.}}{105}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Enzyme aperture dynamics. The catalytic efficiency $k_\mathrm {cat}/K_m$ is plotted as a function of partition depth $n$ (number of hierarchical levels in the active-site partition cell $\mathcal {C}_E$). The efficiency peaks at $n^* = 7$: at smaller $n$ the aperture cell is too large (non-reactive substrates inflate $K_m$); at larger $n$ the cell is too small (the correct substrate is excluded by entropic cost, suppressing $k_\mathrm {cat}$). The inset shows the partition capacity function $C(n) = 2n^2$ used to fit the data. Data points span six orders of magnitude in catalytic rate across diverse enzyme families, validating the intermediate-depth optimum predicted by \Cref {thm:depth-optimum}.}}{106}{figure.4.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Opacity independence of the categorical distance $d_{\mathrm {cat}}$. Pairs of states $(A, B)$ are compared across three conditions: free space, partial opacity, and complete optical blocking. In all three conditions the categorical distance $d_{\mathrm {cat}}(A,B) = |\mathcal {M}(A) - \mathcal {M}(B)|$ is unchanged, confirming \Cref {thm:opacity}: opacity modifies photon propagation geometry but not partition depths, so $d_{\mathrm {cat}}$ is unaffected. The right panel illustrates categorical propagation: states that are categorically close ($d_{\mathrm {cat}}$ small) remain measurably correlated even when separated by an opaque barrier, because the measurement apparatus and target share a small number of intermediate partition cells.}}{133}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Information catalysis. The main panel plots the catalytic efficiency $k_\mathrm {cat}/K_m$ against the reduction in categorical distance $\Delta d_{\mathrm {cat}}= d_{\mathrm {cat}}(S_\mathrm {free}, P) - d_{\mathrm {cat}}(S_\mathrm {bound}, P)$ achieved by enzyme binding. The exponential dependence $k_\mathrm {cat}/K_m \propto \exp (-\delta \cdot d_{\mathrm {cat}}(S,P)/k_{\mathrm {B}}T)$ (\Cref {thm:info-catalysis}) is confirmed across multiple enzyme families. The inset illustrates that binding reduces the effective substrate depth from $\mathcal {M}_S$ to the active-site depth $\mathcal {M}_E$, thereby decreasing the categorical distance traversed in the substrate-to-product transformation. Enzymes act as information catalysts: they reduce the categorical distance of the transformation, not merely its free-energy barrier.}}{136}{figure.5.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The eight cellular oscillator classes with their characteristic frequency ranges and coherence metrics. Each class ($P$, $E$, $C$, $M$, $A$, $G$, Ca, $R$) is shown as a horizontal bar spanning its physiological frequency range on a logarithmic axis from $10^{-5}$~Hz to $10^{15}$~Hz. Colour encodes the coherence metric used to quantify each class's order parameter $\Pi _\mathrm {obs}^k$. Together these eight classes define the complete set of observables required for the cellular coherence index $\eta _{\mathrm {cell}}$ (\Cref {def:coherence-index}) and the Health--Coherence Criterion (\Cref {thm:health-criterion}).}}{141}{figure.5.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Partition geometry for trajectory completion. The left panel shows the trit-state space $\{-1, 0, +1\}^n$ projected onto a two-dimensional slice, with valid trit trajectories (single-flip sequences) indicated by edges. The right panel contrasts the feasible region of biochemical trajectories (a compact subset constrained by Kirchhoff balance, partition depth gradients, and aperture conditions) with the full ambient space $\mathbb {R}^k_+$. The Poincaré recurrence structure forces all trajectories to remain within the feasible region, motivating the trajectory completion formulation over forward simulation.}}{169}{figure.6.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Trajectory completion by the Project-Complete-Compose (PCC) algorithm. Sparse observations $\{x_{t_1}, \ldots , x_{t_m}\}$ (filled circles) are projected to partition cells; the Viterbi algorithm then recovers the MAP trit trajectory (solid path) connecting them under Kirchhoff, thermodynamic, and aperture constraints. The shaded region marks the uncertainty envelope from within-cell degeneracy. The lower panel compares computational cost: PCC ($\mathcal {O}(mk)$, linear) versus forward simulation ($\mathcal {O}(e^{kT})$, exponential).}}{171}{figure.6.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~II localization summary.} \textbf {(A)}~Six arrival-time surfaces (chemical, acoustic, thermal, electromagnetic, vibrational, categorical) intersecting at the reaction site $\mathbf {r}^{*}$. Each surface is drawn at the observed arrival time $T_k^{\mathrm {obs}}$; the intersection region shrinks to sub-nanometre scale as modalities are added. \textbf {(B)}~Positional uncertainty $\sigma _r$ versus number of active modalities, from single-modality diffraction-limited $\sigma \approx 100\,\mathrm {nm}$ to six-modality $\sigma _r \approx 0.05\,\mathrm {nm}$. \textbf {(C)}~Convergence of the iterative refinement algorithm (\Cref {alg:localization}) over 20 iterations for a tissue phantom with one obstructed EM channel. \textbf {(D)}~Categorical arrival-time surface (isopotential of partition depth $\mathcal {M}$) overlaid on the five physical surfaces, demonstrating modality-independence of $v_K$.}}{199}{figure.7.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Six-modality comparison.} Side-by-side visualisation of the six propagation modalities (chemical, acoustic, thermal, electromagnetic, vibrational, and categorical) showing their respective arrival-time surfaces, characteristic speeds, tissue exclusion conditions, and relative positional uncertainty contributions. The categorical modality (K) is the only one with no exclusion factor, penetrating every physical barrier without attenuation.}}{201}{figure.7.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Multimodal localization algorithm result.} Convergence of the iterative gradient-descent localization algorithm (\Cref {eq:gradient-descent}) applied to a tissue phantom. Each panel shows the estimated reaction-site position $\hat {\mathbf {r}}_n$ at successive iterations, along with the residual $\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k w_k(T_k(\hat {\mathbf {r}}_n) - T_k^{\mathrm {obs}})^2$. The algorithm achieves sub-nanometre convergence within 20 iterations, with the categorical modality providing the critical constraint that prevents the estimate from collapsing onto a symmetry axis.}}{210}{figure.7.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional illustration of opacity independence. \textbf {Left}: Free space case ($\tau = 0$). All six arrival-time surfaces intersect at $\mathbf {r}^*$. \textbf {Centre}: Partially opaque tissue ($\tau = 2$). The EM surface is distorted (attenuated by Debye screening outside the 2.5~nm constraint sphere); the optical component of the EM modality is suppressed. The categorical surface $\mathcal {S}_K$ is unchanged. \textbf {Right}: Completely opaque tissue ($\tau \gg 1$). The EM and vibrational modalities may be fully blocked. The acoustic, thermal, chemical, and categorical modalities remain operative; the four-modality intersection still uniquely determines $\mathbf {r}^*$. The categorical surface $\mathcal {S}_K$ is identical in all three panels, confirming \Cref {thm:opacity-proof}.}}{214}{figure.7.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~III summary: Disease.} \textbf {(A)}~Fuzzy membership functions $\mu _{\mathrm {healthy}}(d)$ (green) and $\mu _{\mathrm {diseased}}(d)$ (red) versus distance $d(x, \mathcal {C}_{\mathrm {health}})$ from the health cell. The crossover distance marks the onset of disease ambiguity; the shaded region is the fuzzy disease zone. \textbf {(B)}~Backward trajectory fan: 25 trajectories from diverse starting conditions converging to the health cell (green disc) under the backward propagator $\mathcal {B}$, illustrating reference-free state recovery. \textbf {(C)}~Wegscheider log-holonomy $|H_\ell |$ versus loop length for healthy circuits (green, $\approx 10^{-14}$) and diseased circuits (red, $\approx O(1)$). The gap is the detection signal. \textbf {(D)}~Three-dimensional holonomy landscape $|H_\ell |(\sigma , L)$: holonomy grows as $\sigma \!\sqrt {L}$ with perturbation strength $\sigma $ and loop length $L$.}}{239}{figure.8.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Disease taxonomy by oscillator class.} Classification of pathological states within the eight-dimensional disease vector $\mathbf {D}= (D_P, D_E, D_C, D_M, D_A, D_G, D_{Ca}, D_R)$. Each axis corresponds to one oscillator class; the dominant incoherence $\mathrm {class}(\mathbf {D}) = \arg \max _k D_k$ assigns the disease to its primary mechanistic category. Representative diseases cluster visibly in distinct regions of the taxonomy space.}}{241}{figure.8.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Categorical memory reset.} Illustration of partition memory acquisition and the energy cost of therapeutic reset. The upper panels show the partition depth trajectory $\mathcal {M}(t)$ departing from $\mathcal {C}_{\mathrm {health}}$ at disease onset $t_d$ and failing to return after the perturbation $\delta G \to 0$. The lower panel shows the reset energy $E_{\mathrm {reset}} \geq k_{\mathrm {B}}T\, \Delta \mathcal {M}_{\mathrm {mem}}$ (\Cref {thm:reset-energy}) as a function of accumulated epigenetic partition memory, establishing the thermodynamic cost of reverting entrenched disease states such as cancer cell reprogramming or senescence reversal.}}{244}{figure.8.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Coherence and disease.} Kuramoto order parameter $r(t)$ and pairwise phase coherence $\mathcal {C}(t)$ trajectories for healthy (green, $r > r_c$) and diseased (red, $r < r_c$) cellular oscillator networks. The crossover at the critical coupling threshold $r_c$ marks disease onset as defined by \Cref {def:disease-coherence}. The inset shows the Coherence--Holonomy Relation (\Cref {prop:coherence-holonomy}): $1 - \mathcal {C}(t)$ grows proportionally to the mean Wegscheider log-holonomy $\langle |\mathrm {hol}_\ell |\rangle _\ell $ as disease disrupts detailed balance across circuit loops.}}{252}{figure.8.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Circuit topology and Kirchhoff analogs.} The biochemical circuit $\mathcal {G}= (V, E, w)$ with molecular species as nodes (node voltage = chemical potential $\varphi _i = k_{\mathrm {B}}T \ln c_i/c_{\mathrm {ref}}$) and directed reactions as weighted edges (conductance $w_{ij} = k_{ij}^+ c_j$). Left: graph topology showing hub nodes (large degree) and peripheral nodes. Centre: Kirchhoff Current Law (KCL) balance at each node at steady state. Right: Kirchhoff Voltage Law (KVL) / Wegscheider condition on a representative loop, illustrating the loop holonomy $\mathrm {hol}_\ell = 0$ in the healthy (detailed-balance) state and $\mathrm {hol}_\ell \neq 0$ in the diseased state.}}{272}{figure.9.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Fuzzy state propagation through the biochemical circuit.} Membership functions $\mu _i(c)$ for representative nodes propagate through the circuit via the Zadeh extension principle (sup-min composition, \Cref {eq:fuzzy-kcl}). Left: Gaussian membership functions $\mu _i(c) = \exp (-(c-\bar {c}_i)^2/2\sigma _i^2)$ at input nodes. Centre: propagated membership at intermediate nodes, showing broadening of uncertainty with circuit depth. Right: defuzzified macroscopic potential $\bar {\varphi }_i^{\mathrm {macro}}$ versus the sharp classical value, illustrating that fuzzy averaging preserves the circuit's aggregate thermodynamic quantities while encoding the irreducible concentration uncertainty.}}{278}{figure.9.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Reference-free diagnosis via holonomy and backward-trajectory convergence.} Left: backward trajectory fan --- 25 trajectories from diverse starting conditions converging to the health cell (green disc) under repeated application of the backward propagator $\mathcal {B}$, illustrating that the self-consistent fixed point is recovered without any stored healthy template. Right: Wegscheider log-holonomy $|\mathrm {hol}_\ell |$ versus loop length for healthy circuits (green, $\approx 10^{-14}$) and diseased circuits (red, $O(1)$), showing the diagnostic gap that enables AUC $= 1.0$ classification (\Cref {thm:reference-free}).}}{280}{figure.9.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Signal variance preceding mean shift along a disease trajectory.} Time series of macroscopic signal variance $\mathrm {Var}(x^{\mathrm {macro}}(t))$ (blue) and mean $\langle x^{\mathrm {macro}}(t)\rangle $ (orange) during disease progression parameterised by increasing loop holonomy $|\mathrm {hol}_\ell (t)|$. The variance becomes detectably elevated before the mean departs from the healthy reference range, consistent with the variance--holonomy relation $\mathrm {Var}(x^{\mathrm {macro}}) = k_{\mathrm {B}}T / |\mathrm {hol}_\ell |$ and \Cref {prop:variance-precedes}. The dashed vertical line marks the mean-shift onset time $t_d$; the solid vertical line marks the earlier variance-detection threshold.}}{287}{figure.9.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~IV summary: Therapeutic Intervention.} \textbf {(A)}~$\ell _1$ versus $\ell _2$ norm balls in $\mathbb {R}^2$. The constraint hyperplane (red dotted) is tangent to the $\ell _1$ ball at a coordinate-axis vertex (sparse solution, blue dot), while the $\ell _2$ solution (red dot) is dense, illustrating why the $\ell _1$ LP recovers sparse drug combinations. \textbf {(B)}~Mean LP solution sparsity versus number of therapeutic constraints (loops), averaged over 30 trials per point. Sparsity increases with constraints as the feasible set shrinks, confirming that more targeted therapy is achievable with more holonomy constraints. \textbf {(C)}~Reversibility scatter: $\log |\det H|$ versus $\log $ LP residual. Invertible circuits (green, $\det H \neq 0$) achieve zero residual; singular circuits (red, $\det H = 0$) cannot be restored by any drug combination (\Cref {thm:reversibility}). \textbf {(D)}~Three-dimensional restoration surface: therapeutic quality versus drug count $n_\mathrm {drugs}$ and total drug load $\|\bm {\eta }\|$. Restoration saturates near $1$ with sufficient drug count, confirming the adequacy of the sparse LP solution.}}{299}{figure.10.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Self-consistency and the therapeutic criterion.} Relationship between the Wegscheider self-consistency condition $H_\ell = \mathrm {Id}$ and the therapeutic restoration of healthy loop closure. Left: holonomy magnitude $|H_\ell |$ for diseased loops before (red) and after (green) application of the optimal drug vector $\bm {\eta }^*$. Right: diagnostic AUC comparison between template-based comparison (AUC $= 0.896$), local edge inspection (AUC $= 0.52$), and holonomy self-consistency (AUC $= 1.00$), confirming that the therapeutic criterion (\Cref {thm:therapeutic-criterion}) targets the uniquely complete disease signature.}}{301}{figure.10.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Therapeutic trajectory in circuit state space.} Three-dimensional restoration surface showing therapeutic quality $(H_\ell \to \mathrm {Id}$ restoration fraction) as a joint function of drug count $n_{\mathrm {drugs}}$ and total drug load $\|\bm {\eta }\|_1$. The surface saturates near full restoration with the sparse LP optimum $\bm {\eta }^*$, confirming that minimum side-effect burden (minimum $\|\bm {\eta }^*\|_1$) achieves the same restoration quality as any dense combination. Overlaid trajectories show individual disease circuits from the validation set (\Cref {sec:validation}) converging to the healthy basin under LP-guided therapy.}}{304}{figure.10.3}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Sparse LP drug design for multi-loop holonomy restoration.} Left: $\ell _1$ versus $\ell _2$ norm balls in $\mathbb {R}^2$, illustrating why the LP objective recovers sparse drug combinations: the therapeutic constraint hyperplane is generically tangent to the $\ell _1$ ball at a coordinate-axis vertex (sparse solution), while the $\ell _2$ solution is dense. Right: mean LP solution sparsity $\|\bm {\eta }^*\|_0$ versus number of diseased loops $|\mathcal {L}_D|$, averaged over 30 trials per point, confirming that the minimum effective drug combination contains at least one agent per diseased loop (\Cref {prop:polypharmacy}) and that polypharmacy emerges from LP geometry rather than empirical synergy screens.}}{341}{figure.11.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Validation record across four disease case studies.} \textbf {(A)} AUC comparison: holonomy classifier (AUC $= 1.00$) versus template comparison (AUC $= 0.896$) versus local edge inspection (AUC $= 0.52$) across the four case studies. \textbf {(B)} LP sparsity: $\|\bm {\eta }^*\|_0$ for each case matches the number of diseased loops. \textbf {(C)} Pharmacological salvageability: $\det (H_\ell )$ correctly predicts drug response in all four cases. \textbf {(D)} Computational timing: LP solving time versus circuit size.}}{346}{figure.11.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Part~V summary: Synthesis.} \textbf {(A)}~Postulate elimination: ten central assumptions of the standard homeostasis/pharmacology/systems-biology framework are each replaced by consequences of the two foundational axioms. Red crosses mark eliminated postulates; green ticks mark the axiomatic replacements. \textbf {(B)}~Diagnostic AUC across three strategies: template comparison (AUC $= 0.896$, reference-dependent), local edge inspection (AUC $= 0.52$, random classifier), and Wegscheider holonomy (AUC $= 1.00$, reference-free). Holonomy is the unique viable diagnostic. \textbf {(C)}~Epistemic floor hierarchy $\beta = \sigma /\!\sqrt {N}$ across biological scales from molecule ($N \sim 10^2$) to organism ($N \sim 10^{12}$), illustrating that the framework's floor positivity holds universally across all scales. \textbf {(D)}~Three-dimensional synthesis surface: $\log _{10}\mathcal {S}_{\mathrm {flat}}$ as a joint function of molecular count $\log _{10} N$ (cell size) and ensemble size $n_\mathrm {cells}$, unifying individual cell blindness and group blindness into a single two-parameter landscape.}}{369}{figure.12.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.2}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Hub vulnerability analysis connecting multiple chapters.} Scale-free network topology of the biochemical circuit $\mathcal {G}$ with hub nodes highlighted (degree $d_i > \bar {d} + 2\sigma _d$). The hub fractional holonomy contribution $f_h = \binom {d_h}{2}/|L|$ is shown for each hub, revealing that single-lesion events at high-degree hubs (ATP, NADH, acetyl-CoA) simultaneously disrupt a macroscopic fraction of the circuit's loops. This analysis bridges the disease-circuit framework of \Cref {ch:fuzzy-circuits}, the therapeutic hierarchy of \Cref {ch:intervention}, and the scale-free network vulnerability literature.}}{375}{figure.12.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.3}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Overall validation panel across the framework.} Consolidated validation results for the partition-holonomy framework across four disease circuits. For each circuit, the panel shows: measured holonomy $|H_\ell |$ before and after LP-guided therapy; LP solution sparsity $\|\bm {\eta }^*\|_0$; pharmacological salvageability ($\det (H_\ell ) \neq 0$ or $= 0$); and agreement between the LP-predicted optimal drug combination and the clinical standard-of-care. All four cases pass.}}{383}{figure.12.3}%
